.TH b4-decrypt 1  "b4 0.1.0" 
.SH NAME
b4\-decrypt \- Decrypt a vault into a DuckDB database
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBb4 decrypt\fR [\fB\-\-db\fR] [\fB\-\-password\-stdin\fR] [\fB\-o\fR|\fB\-\-output\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-header\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-V\fR|\fB\-\-version\fR] <\fIVAULT\fR> [\fIOUT\fR] 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Decrypt a vault into a DuckDB database
.PP
Decrypt VAULT and write its accounts, bookings, payees, categories, standing orders, payments and securities data into a DuckDB database.
.PP
The database is written to a temporary file next to its final place and renamed when it is complete, so a reader never sees half a database. An existing database is replaced: running decrypt again is how the database is refreshed. The file is created with mode 0600.
.PP
The password comes from the first of these that applies: standard input with \-\-password\-stdin, the environment variable B4_PASSWORD, a prompt without echo on the terminal, or, when b4 has no terminal, a program that asks for it. It cannot be given as an argument and is never stored.
.PP
Without a terminal, as when a desktop launcher, a scheduler or an agent starts b4, a program can ask instead, typically in a dialog: the one named by the environment variable B4_ASKPASS, or else, in a graphical session (WAYLAND_DISPLAY or DISPLAY is set), the one named by SSH_ASKPASS, unless SSH_ASKPASS_REQUIRE is `never`. The variable holds the name or path of one program, without arguments. The program gets the question as its only argument and no standard input, and prints the password as one line on standard output; a status other than 0 means that the user cancelled.
.PP
As long as b4 has a terminal it asks there, also when standard input is redirected, and a prompt ended with Ctrl\-D is not followed by a dialog. `setsid \-w b4 decrypt ...` starts b4 without a terminal.
.PP
The password goes from that program to b4 through a pipe: it does not appear in the arguments, the environment or the output of whatever started b4. This does not protect it from the one who starts b4, who can name any program in B4_ASKPASS. b4 runs that program as it is and cannot check it.
.PP
Prefer the prompt or \-\-password\-stdin. An environment variable can be read by every other process of the same user while b4 runs, is passed on to the programs a shell starts, and stays set in the shell that exported it. b4 removes B4_PASSWORD from its own environment as soon as it has read it, but cannot hide what it was started with.
.PP
One line per table and any warnings go to stderr. The vault is opened read\-only, and the bank access data in it is skipped without being read.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-password\-stdin\fR
Read the password from the first line of standard input

One line ending (LF or CRLF) is removed, nothing else. An empty line is an empty password.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print version
.TP
<\fIVAULT\fR>
The vault file (.sub) to read
.TP
[\fIOUT\fR]
The database file to write [default: the file of \-\-db]
.SH "GLOBAL OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-\-db\fR \fI<PATH>\fR [default: ./b4.duckdb]
Database file: written by `decrypt`, read by every other command
.RS
May also be specified with the \fBB4_DB\fR environment variable. 
.RE
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-output\fR \fI<FORMAT>\fR
Output format [default: table]

table is made for reading: line breaks, control characters and text direction overrides in the data are replaced. csv, jsonl and json are data: written to a file or a pipe they are exact, byte for byte. Only when they are written to a terminal are the characters a terminal acts on (escape sequences, text direction overrides) made harmless: csv shows U+FFFD in their place, json and jsonl write them as \\uXXXX escapes.

In json and jsonl a number that a double cannot hold exactly is a string of its digits: an integer beyond 2^53, a decimal of more than 15 significant digits.
.br

.br
\fIPossible values:\fR
.RS 14
.IP \(bu 2
table: Aligned text, fitted to the terminal
.IP \(bu 2
json: One JSON array of objects
.IP \(bu 2
jsonl: One JSON object per line
.IP \(bu 2
csv: Comma\-separated values with a header line
.RE
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-header\fR
Leave out the header line of table and csv output
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
Do not print informational messages to stderr

Silences progress and summary lines and the "no rows" note. Warnings and errors are still printed.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
b4 decrypt \(ti/Banking/Household.sub
    Ask for the password and write ./b4.duckdb.
b4 decrypt \(ti/Banking/Household.sub \(ti/.cache/b4/household.duckdb
    Write the database somewhere else.
pass show banking4 | b4 decrypt \-\-password\-stdin \(ti/Banking/Household.sub
    Take the password from a password manager.
B4_DB=\(ti/.cache/b4.duckdb b4 decrypt \(ti/Banking/Household.sub
    Refresh the database that the other commands use.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
\fBb4\fR(1)
